# LandedCompare — client configuration
#
# Copy to `.env.local` (which is gitignored) and fill in the values for the
# project this build should talk to.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What belongs in this file, and what must never
#
# docs/CLOUD_MULTIUSER_ARCHITECTURE.md §19. Every `VITE_` variable ends up in
# the JavaScript bundle BY DEFINITION — that is what the prefix means — and a
# desktop binary is a file on someone's computer, so anything compiled into it
# is public.
#
#   MAY be here:    the project URL, the PUBLISHABLE key (sb_publishable_…)
#   MUST NEVER be:  the secret key (sb_secret_…), the legacy service-role key,
#                   the database password, a management access token
#
# A publishable key is not a secret and is not treated as one. It grants exactly
# what the `anon` and `authenticated` roles are granted, and `anon` is granted
# NOTHING on any business object in any schema. Extracting it from a bundle, a
# network trace or a decompiled binary gives an attacker the capabilities of a
# logged-out visitor — which is the test this whole design has to pass.
#
# A secret key bypasses every row-level policy in the database. `npm run build`
# scans the production bundle for secret key material and FAILS on a hit,
# because that mistake is catastrophic and silent: a bundle carrying a secret
# key works perfectly and hands unrestricted database access to anyone who
# opens the network tab.
#
# There is NO server secret to set by hand. The Edge Functions read the key the
# platform injects (`SUPABASE_SECRET_KEYS`, or the legacy
# `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`) — docs/CLOUD_MULTIUSER_ARCHITECTURE.md §19. A copy
# created with `supabase secrets set` would only be a second, drifting copy of
# the most dangerous credential in the system.
#
# The build and the runtime check refuse `sb_secret_…` AND any JWT-form key
# whose decoded role is not `anon` — a legacy service-role key never contains
# the text "service_role" in readable form.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local development
#
# `supabase start` prints a URL and a publishable key. They are identical on
# every machine, are published in Supabase's own documentation, and open a
# throwaway database listening on 127.0.0.1 — so they are not secret, and the
# local stack is never exposed publicly.
#
#   VITE_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
#   VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=<the PUBLISHABLE_KEY from `supabase status`>

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
